#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# reduce

#% text
# `reduce(cond, x, reals)` gives the condition `cond` on the real variable `x`, written as intervals: `x < a`, `b < x and x <= c` and so on, joined by `or`.

#% text
# `reduce(cond, [x, y, ...], dom)` solves for several variables over `reals`, `integers` or `complexes`, giving cylindrical conditions: one on `x`, then one on `y` for that `x`, and so on.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `cond` is built from polynomial or rational equations and inequalities with `and`, `or`, `not` and the quantifiers `exists` and `for_all`.

#% item
# Over the reals, several variables are handled by cylindrical algebraic decomposition (see `cylindrical_decomposition`); `abs`, `max` and `min` are split into cases there. In one variable the condition is polynomial or rational, possibly with `exp`, `log`, `sin`, `cos` or `tan` of the variable itself (see Possible issues).

#% item
# Over the integers, linear and quadratic equations in two variables give their families of solutions with an integer parameter `c(1)` (Pell equations included); bounded problems give the list of solutions.

#% item
# Over the complexes, equations and `!=` in several variables or with parameters give a triangular description: each variable is solved given the earlier ones.

#% item
# Parameters that are not among the variables give the cases in which solutions exist.

#% item
# Without a domain, equations alone (and `!=`) in one variable are solved over the complexes; a condition with an inequality is over the reals.

#% item
# `reduce(eqs, x, modulus: n)` gives the solutions of polynomial equations modulo `n`.

#% item
# The endpoints are exact, with `root` objects where they are not rational or radical.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
reduce(x^2 < 4, x, reals)
#% output
#= -2 < x and x < 2

#% cell
reduce(x^2 - 3*x + 2 >= 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= x <= 1 or 2 <= x

#% cell
reduce((x - 1)/(x + 2) > 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= x < -2 or 1 < x

#% cell
reduce(x^3 == x, x, reals)
#% output
#= x == -1 or x == 0 or x == 1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several conditions:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 > 1 and x < 3, x, reals)
#% output
#= x < -1 or 1 < x and x < 3

#% text
# A condition that always holds, and one that never holds:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 + 1 > 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= true

#% cell
reduce(x^2 < 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= false

#% text
# Roots that are not radicals:

#% cell
reduce(x^5 - x - 1 == 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= x == root(&(-1 - &1 + &1^5), 1)

#% text
# Two variables over the reals:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 + y^2 < 1 and y > x, [x, y], reals)
#% output
#= -1 < x and x <= (-1/2)*2^(1/2) and -(1 - x^2)^(1/2) < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2) or (-1/2)*2^(1/2) < x and x < (1/2)*2^(1/2) and x < y and y < (1 - x^2)^(1/2)

#% text
# A quantifier is eliminated:

#% cell
reduce(exists(y, x == y^2), x, reals)
#% output
#= x >= 0

#% text
# A parameter gives the case in which there are solutions:

#% cell
reduce(a*x^2 + 1 == 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= a < 0 and (x == (-a)^(1/2)/a or x == -(-a)^(1/2)/a)

#% text
# Integer solutions of a bounded problem:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 - 2*y^2 == 1 and x > 0 and x < 20, [x, y], integers)
#% output
#= x == 1 and y == 0 or x == 3 and y == -2 or x == 3 and y == 2 or x == 17 and y == -12 or x == 17 and y == 12

#% text
# Complex solutions:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 + 1 == 0, x, complexes)
#% output
#= x == -im or x == im

#% cell
reduce(x^2 + y^2 == 1 and x == y, [x, y], complexes)
#% output
#= (x == (-1/2)*2^(1/2) or x == (1/2)*2^(1/2)) and y == x

#% text
# Equations without a domain are over the complexes:

#% cell
reduce(x^3 == 1, x)
#% output
#= x == 1 or x == -(-1)^(1/3) or x == (-1)^(2/3)

#% text
# Modulo a prime, and with an absolute value over the reals:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 == 2, x, modulus: 7)
#% output
#= x == 3 or x == 4

#% cell
reduce(abs(x - 1) < 2, x, reals)
#% output
#= -1 < x and x < 3

#% text
# Parameters: the cases are given as conditions on them:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 > a, x, reals)
#% output
#= a < 0 or a == 0 and (x < 0 or x > 0) or a > 0 and (x < -a^(1/2) or x > a^(1/2))

#% cell
reduce(a*x > 1, x, reals)
#% output
#= a < 0 and x < a^-1 or a > 0 and x > a^-1

#% text
# Quantifiers over the reals:

#% cell
reduce(exists(x, x^2 + b*x + 1 == 0), b, reals)
#% output
#= b <= -2 or b >= 2

#% cell
reduce(for_all(x, x^2 + b*x + 1 > 0), b, reals)
#% output
#= -2 < b and b < 2

#% text
# Conditions that combine into one, and a region in the plane:

#% cell
reduce(x > 1 and x > 2, x, reals)
#% output
#= 2 < x

#% cell
reduce(abs(x) + abs(y) <= 1 and y > 0, [x, y], reals)
#% output
#= -1 < x and x <= 0 and 0 < y and y <= 1 + x or 0 < x and x < 1 and 0 < y and y <= 1 - x

#% text
# Integer solutions of a linear condition:

#% cell
reduce(2*x + 3*y == 7 and x >= 0 and y >= 0, [x, y], integers)
#% output
#= x == 2 and y == 1

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# `assumptions:` restricts the parameters:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 > a, x, reals, assumptions: a > 0)
#% output
#= a > 0 and (x < -a^(1/2) or x > a^(1/2))

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The equality case gives the real roots:

#% cell
[reduce(x^3 - 2*x == 0, x, reals), solve(x^3 - 2*x == 0, x)]
#% output
#= [x == 0 or x == -2^(1/2) or x == 2^(1/2), [[x -> 0], [x -> -2^(1/2)], [x -> 2^(1/2)]]]

#% text
# The condition holds exactly where the original does; a point in the region gives `true`:

#% cell
reduce(x^3 - x - 1 > 0, x, reals)
#% output
#= root(&(-1 - &1 + &1^3), 1) < x

#% cell
reduce(x^3 - x - 1 > 0, x, reals) /. x -> 2
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The end points are exact; `n` gives their values:

#% cell
n(reduce(x^3 - x - 1 == 0, x, reals))
#% output
#= x == 1.324717957244746

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A condition on a function of the unknown itself (`exp`, `log`, `sin`, `cos`, `tan` of the variable) next to rational conditions is solved by mapping the answer back through the function's inverse on each monotone branch; a periodic function needs bounds on the variable:

#% cell
reduce(exp(x) > 1, x, reals)
#% output
#= 0 < x

#% cell
reduce(log(x) < 1, x, reals)
#% output
#= 0 < x and x < e

#% cell
reduce(sin(x) > 1/2 and 0 < x and x < 2*pi, x, reals)
#% output
#= (1/6)*pi < x and x < (5/6)*pi

#% cell
reduce(sin(x) == 0 and 0 < x and x < 4*pi, x, reals)
#% output
#= x == pi or x == 2*pi or x == 3*pi

#% text
# Powers of one exponential with rational multiples of the unknown are reduced through that exponential:

#% cell
reduce(exp(x) + exp(-x) > 3, x, reals)
#% output
#= x < log((1/2)*(3 - 5^(1/2))) or log((1/2)*(3 + 5^(1/2))) < x

#% text
# A periodic function without bounds over the reals, and a transcendental condition with a parameter, are not solved: the call returns a failure and `solve` or `find_root` can be used for the points:

#% cell
reduce(sin(x) == 0, x, reals)
#% output
#! failure(unsolved, reduce, "reduce handles one real variable with rational coefficients")

#% cell
reduce(exp(x) > a, x, reals)
#% output
#! failure(unsolved, reduce, "reduce handles one real variable with rational coefficients")

#% text
# Over the complexes a transcendental equation is solved with a generated integer parameter:

#% cell
reduce(exp(x) == x, x, complexes)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(x == -product_log(c(1), -1), element(c(1), integers))

#% text
# `working_precision:` is not an option, and the domain `rationals` gives `false` for an equation with only irrational solutions; other domains for inequalities are refused:

#% cell
reduce(x^2 < 2, x, reals, working_precision: 30)
#% output
#! failure(argument, reduce, "expected a condition, a variable and reals")

#% cell
reduce(x^2 == 2, x, rationals)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
reduce(x^2 < 2, x, integers)
#% output
#! failure(argument, reduce, "reduce works over the reals")

#% text
# The endpoints are exact; `n` gives them to any number of digits:

#% cell
n(reduce(x^2 > 2, x, reals), 30)
#% output
#= x < -1.4142135623730950488016887242096981 or 1.4142135623730950488016887242096981 < x

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `solve`, `cylindrical_decomposition`, `resolve`, `find_instance`, `minimize`, `roots`, `assuming`
